lesson:
You've used generics since Level 1: `ArrayList<String>`, `HashMap<String, Integer>`. The `<...>` part is a **type parameter**: the list is written once by the Java team, and you choose what it holds. Now you'll write your own.

**Why generics exist.** Without them, a list would hold `Object`, and every time you took something out you'd cast it and hope: `String s = (String) list.get(0);`. Put a number in by mistake and the program crashes later, far from the bug. With `List<String>`, the compiler refuses the number on the spot. Generics move mistakes from run time to compile time, and save you the casts.

**A generic class** names its type parameters after the class name:

```
public class Pair<A, B> {
    private final A first;
    private final B second;
    public Pair(A first, B second) { this.first = first; this.second = second; }
    public A first() { return first; }
    public B second() { return second; }
}

Pair<String, Integer> p = new Pair<>("maize", 40);
int kg = p.second();          // no cast needed
```

`A` and `B` are placeholders; each use fills them in. `new Pair<>(...)` (the "diamond") lets Java work out the types.

**A generic method** declares its own type parameter before the return type:

```
static <T> T firstOr(List<T> items, T fallback) {
    return items.isEmpty() ? fallback : items.get(0);
}
```

**Bounded types.** To find the biggest item, you must be able to compare items. Not every type can. So you put a **bound** on T:

```
static <T extends Comparable<T>> T max(List<T> items) {
    T best = items.get(0);
    for (T x : items) if (x.compareTo(best) > 0) best = x;
    return best;
}
```

`<T extends Comparable<T>>` reads "any T that can compare itself to another T". Integer, Double and String all qualify, so one method works for all of them. Inside, you may call `compareTo` because the bound promises it exists. (In a bound, `extends` is used for interfaces too.)

Things to know:
- Type parameters must be classes: `List<Integer>`, never `List<int>`.
- Name them with single capital letters by habit: `T` (type), `E` (element), `K`/`V` (key/value).
- You can't do `new T()`; Java erases T when it runs.
- `List<Slime>` is **not** a `List<Enemy>`, even though a Slime is an Enemy, because otherwise you could add an Archer to a list of slimes.

In real programs, generics are everywhere: a game's `ObjectPool<Bullet>`, a `Cache<K, V>`, an inventory `Counter<Item>`, a robot's `RingBuffer<Reading>`. Write it once, use it for any type, and keep the compiler on your side.

